  "id": "78",
  "name": "Employment activities",
  "section": "O",
  "summary": "Staffing, recruitment and HR-outsourcing firms use AI for candidate matching, screening, sourcing and recruiter productivity, and sell AI-skilled talent, reskilling and data-annotation workforces. They flag AI as a structural threat: lower demand for white-collar, entry-level and technology hiring, clients recruiting in-house with AI tools, and AI-native platforms disintermediating agencies. Legal exposure is specific: automated employment decision tool laws, bias audits, EEOC and Title VII liability, the EU AI Act high-risk employment category, algorithmic management rules, plus deepfake and AI-generated candidate fraud.",
